  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Unit: YM-000026, lattice existence construction.

    <item>Status: draft; independent mathematical/source review pending.

    <item>Imported Lean revision: <verbatim|3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464>,
    as recorded in <verbatim|source/upstream.json>.

    <item>Prose: <verbatim|manuscript/existence/free-kernel-components.tex>.

    <item>Preceding conditional conversion:
    <verbatim|manuscript/existence/free-kernel-estimates.tex>.
  </itemize-minus>

  This chapter reorganizes the actual zero/nonzero branch supplier into
  ordinary estimates. It identifies the component decay powers and explains
  the quantitative closure. The history-indexed train induction is given as
  an explicit geometric side-ratio induction, including the coefficient-one
  principal-plank lower bound, the nozzle recursion, and the separate
  patch-mass and train-smooth factors.

  <section|Exact source objects and bounds><label|exact-source-objects-and-bounds>

  <verbatim|Moderate w f> in <verbatim|YM.Next.Free.Moderate>
  (<verbatim|Lean/YM/Next/FREE_KERNELS/Moderate.lean>) means that one common
  positive complex-cube width and one common constant bound every member of
  the family by <verbatim|C*w i>; the members are holomorphic on that cube.
  <verbatim|Firm w f> supplies a lower bound <verbatim|c*w i> on the
  corresponding real enlarged cube. <verbatim|Moderate.kept> proves the
  common-cube lower bound needed to take reciprocals, with radius

  <verbatim|min (\<eta\>/4) (min \<theta\> (c*\<eta\>/(8*C)))>

  and lower constant <verbatim|c/2>. <verbatim|Moderate.inv> then gives the
  reciprocal estimate. The article's common-cube shrink argument is this
  quantitative operation, not an unquantified pointwise continuity argument.

  <block|<tformat|<twith|table-width|1par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-valign|bottom>|<table|<row|<cell|<strong|Mathematical
  receipt>>|<cell|<strong|Exact declaration(s)>>|<cell|<strong|Path>>|<cell|<strong|Source
  proof mechanism>>>|<row|<cell|One-coordinate alias upper/lower geometry and
  box decay>|<cell|<verbatim|alias_rail>; <verbatim|scaled_alias_upper>;
  <verbatim|scaled_alias_lower>>|<cell|<verbatim|Lean/YM/Next/FREE_KERNELS/Lines.lean>;
  <verbatim|Chip.lean>>|<cell|<verbatim|rail_chip> relates normalized
  geometric sum to the chip; the chip has upper/lower comparison to its
  argument on the fixed disk; a nonzero centered lift forces scaled alias
  size comparable to its lift. The centered phase in <verbatim|plank> is
  uniformly bounded.>>|<row|<cell|Cumulative mass upper
  bound>|<cell|<verbatim|Flight.bills>; <verbatim|Train.smooth_upper>;
  <verbatim|Train.area_diff>>|<cell|<verbatim|Buffer.lean>;
  <verbatim|Train.lean>>|<cell|Four coordinate box factors multiply; each
  costs <verbatim|(1+\|lift_a\|)^-1>; raising to stage <verbatim|j> yields
  <verbatim|decay j>. <verbatim|area_diff> gives
  holomorphy.>>|<row|<cell|Cumulative mass lower bound at the
  root>|<cell|<verbatim|Flight.bills_firm>;
  <verbatim|Train.area_at_root>>|<cell|<verbatim|Buffer.lean>;
  <verbatim|Train.lean>>|<cell|Principal box lower bound on the real disk,
  multiplied over the four axes and raised to <verbatim|j>.>>|<row|<cell|Rail
  upper at alias and lower at the zero root>|<cell|<verbatim|Train.rail_cost>;
  <verbatim|Reserve.rail_cost>; <verbatim|rail_root>>|<cell|<verbatim|Train.lean>;
  <verbatim|Reserve.lean>>|<cell|Alias rail upper is priced by <verbatim|(up
  l a)^-1>; zero-root lower is <verbatim|plankMin = min 1
  (meter.low/meter.high)> on the disk, from
  <verbatim|rail_lower>.>>|<row|<cell|Flux
  receipt>|<cell|<verbatim|Flight.flux_budget>;
  <verbatim|Flight.hinges_budget>>|<cell|<verbatim|Buffer.lean>>|<cell|Mass
  receipt times the finite exterior mask of rail entries, followed by reverse
  and transpose. The extra rail factors are bounded by one in the alias
  price.>>|<row|<cell|Signal receipt>|<cell|<verbatim|Flight.sig_budget>;
  <verbatim|Flight.sigQ_budget>; <verbatim|scaled_seed>>|<cell|<verbatim|Buffer.lean>;
  <verbatim|Chip.lean>>|<cell|The signal is exterior multiplication by the
  scaled-seed vector. Each nonzero matrix entry inserts one coordinate, so it
  costs one factor <verbatim|grade(l)> for every input degree <verbatim|r>;
  see <verbatim|sig_budget>, <verbatim|sigQ_budget>, and
  <verbatim|scaled_seed>.>>|<row|<cell|Train smooth upper/lower
  bounds>|<cell|<verbatim|Train.smooth_cost>; <verbatim|Train.smooth_lower>;
  <verbatim|Train.extra_cube>; <verbatim|Train.extra_root>>|<cell|<verbatim|Train.lean>>|<cell|Induction
  over histories. The bind identity factors one new cumulative box and the
  preceding smooth factor. Coarse dilation is at least three and stage
  increments are zero or one; upper bounds use <verbatim|area_height>, lower
  bounds use <verbatim|area_at_root>.>>|<row|<cell|Directional drive and
  lid>|<cell|<verbatim|Train.drive_prop>; <verbatim|Train.drive_yen>;
  <verbatim|Train.lid_cost>; <verbatim|Train.lid_root>>|<cell|<verbatim|Reserve.lean>>|<cell|Smooth
  factor times a rail; lower bound multiplies smooth and zero-root rail lower
  bounds. The lid is the product of the drive at opposite momenta, so its
  positive lower bound squares.>>|<row|<cell|Uniform noise
  drain>|<cell|<verbatim|Train.feed_budget>; <verbatim|Train.tap_safe>;
  <verbatim|Train.noise_budget>; <verbatim|Train.drain_reserve>>|<cell|<verbatim|Reserve.lean>>|<cell|Each
  feed is a normalized finite alias sum; drive products are summed with the
  uniform <verbatim|decay 2> residue budget. History noise is priced by the
  sum of inverse squared cumulative side lengths; its nozzle cost is at most
  two by induction and side length at least three.>>|<row|<cell|Bulb and
  bend>|<cell|<verbatim|Train.bulb_budget>; <verbatim|Train.bulb_firm>;
  <verbatim|Train.bend_budget>; <verbatim|Train.bend_firm>;
  <verbatim|Train.lift_bend>; <verbatim|Train.bend_strength>>|<cell|<verbatim|Bend.lean>>|<cell|Bulb
  is lid plus divisor times drain. The lid lower bound and nonnegative real
  drain give firmness; divisor and drain upper bounds give the complex-cube
  budget. The identity <verbatim|divisor*bend = sum(jewel/bulb)> and
  seed-square lower/upper bounds yield a positive bend lower bound from a
  uniform bulb upper bound. <verbatim|Moderate.kept> permits reciprocal use
  on a smaller cube.>>|<row|<cell|Nonzero blow-up lower and
  inverse>|<cell|<verbatim|blow_control>; <verbatim|blow_firm>;
  <verbatim|divisor_real_bounds>; <verbatim|scaled_seed>>|<cell|<verbatim|Chip.lean>>|<cell|The
  divisor is the sum of four squared seed magnitudes. One nonzero lift
  coordinate has seed at least a constant times its lift; the selected square
  gives a lower bound proportional to <verbatim|grade(l)^2>. The blow-up is
  side-length squared times the divisor.>>|<row|<cell|Port vector and
  reciprocal port normalizer>|<cell|<verbatim|Flight.portD_budget>;
  <verbatim|Flight.portR_budget>; <verbatim|Train.bulb_budget>;
  <verbatim|Train.bend_budget>; <verbatim|blow_firm>>|<cell|<verbatim|Buffer.lean>;
  <verbatim|Chip.lean>; <verbatim|Bend.lean>>|<cell|Bulb divided by blow-up
  has price <verbatim|grade(l)^-2>; reciprocal port normalizer has price
  <verbatim|grade(l)^-4>. The statements are on a common cube by
  <verbatim|Moderate.inv>.>>|<row|<cell|Tail and regular
  auxiliaries>|<cell|<verbatim|Flight.tail_budget>;
  <verbatim|Flight.abide_budget>; <verbatim|Flight.abide_firm>;
  <verbatim|Flight.hills_budget>; <verbatim|divisor_control>>|<cell|<verbatim|Buffer.lean>;
  <verbatim|Chip.lean>>|<cell|The nonzero alias terms are summed using
  <verbatim|decay_cost>; tail factors include two mass weights and port
  weight, so the residual sum is bounded by the uniform exponent-two residue
  sum. Divisor is a finite sum of seed products.>>|<row|<cell|Pulse and
  vault>|<cell|<verbatim|heart_budget>; <verbatim|pulse_pays>;
  <verbatim|vault_sources>; <verbatim|pulse_sources>; <verbatim|bend_budget>;
  <verbatim|bulb_budget>>|<cell|<verbatim|Heart.lean>>|<cell|Diagonal
  exterior mask of reciprocals/products of bulbs, scaled by
  reciprocal/product of bends; source reciprocals use the real firmness plus
  analytic-cube bound.>>|<row|<cell|Zero branch>|<cell|<verbatim|Flight.copper_budget>>|<cell|<verbatim|Cable.lean>>|<cell|<verbatim|foot_budget>
  times order-two flux plus the brace/latch/mouth term minus pulse/latch/tail
  correction, multiplied by the fixed-scale dual exterior multiplier. Each
  factor has unit alias price.>>|<row|<cell|Nonzero
  branch>|<cell|<verbatim|Flight.stake_budget>;
  <verbatim|Flight.levy_budget>>|<cell|<verbatim|Cable.lean>>|<cell|First
  summand multiplies wing pulse, scaled signal, port mask, order-one flux and
  fixed-scale multiplier. Second multiplies levy, order-two flux, and notch.
  Their prices are <verbatim|grade(l) * grade(l)^-2 * decay j(l)> and
  <verbatim|decay j(l)>, respectively; the former is weakened to
  <verbatim|decay j(l)> using <verbatim|grade(l)\<geq\>1>.>>|<row|<cell|Full
  wire receipt>|<cell|<verbatim|Flight.wire_budget>>|<cell|<verbatim|Cable.lean>>|<cell|Partition
  aliases into zero and nonzero; invoke copper or stake. <verbatim|Pay.times>
  bounds each fixed entry by the finite intermediate type sum;
  <verbatim|Moderate.add> handles sums/subtractions.>>>>>

  Here <verbatim|grade(l)=1+max_a \|lift(l)_a\|>, <verbatim|decay
  j(l)=mix(l)^-j>, and <verbatim|mix(l)=\<big-prod\>_a(1+\|lift(l)_a\|)>. The
  nonzero branch's first term has total weight <verbatim|grade(l) *
  grade(l)^(-2) * mix(l)^(-j)>, which is at most <verbatim|mix(l)^(-j)>
  because <verbatim|grade(l)\<geq\>1>. Its second term has weight
  <verbatim|grade(l)^(-4) * mix(l)^(-j)>, which is also at most
  <verbatim|mix(l)^(-j)>. The formal branch closure uses these inequalities
  (and in the Levy estimate uses the two mass weights before raising its
  price to one).

  <section|Source chain into the exported tower
  receipt><label|source-chain-into-the-exported-tower-receipt>

  The thread <verbatim|Flight.wire> is defined by its zero/nonzero branch in
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Next/FREE_KERNELS/Wire.lean>. For arbitrary index type
  <verbatim|\<iota\>>, flight family <verbatim|F : \<iota\> \<rightarrow\>
  Flight j t>, and selected alias <verbatim|l : \<forall\> i, Site (F i).L>,
  <verbatim|Flight.wire_budget> assumes only <verbatim|k\<gtr\>0>,
  <verbatim|4\<leq\>j>, and <verbatim|4\<leq\>t> and proves an entrywise
  <verbatim|Pay> receipt with alias weight <verbatim|decay j (l i)>. The
  proof calls <verbatim|copper_budget> on zero aliases and
  <verbatim|stake_budget> otherwise. <verbatim|Pay> packages the finite
  matrix-entrywise bounds uniformly over all such choices.

  The proved overlap identity is <verbatim|Secured.relay>, instantiated for
  the wire by <verbatim|secure_both>; it says that shifting momentum by a
  reciprocal vector is compensated by adding the corresponding wrap residue
  to the alias. Reality is the separate <verbatim|Secured.pearl> law.
  <verbatim|Secured.propels> converts the weighted entry bound to a
  spread/alias-average cost. Its proof uses <verbatim|differential_bound>
  (each finite difference adds one scale inverse and one alias-grade factor),
  <verbatim|residue_cost> (the exponential phase is bounded uniformly), and
  <verbatim|decay_cost> plus <verbatim|absorbs> for the alias sum.
  <verbatim|enters> and <verbatim|traverse> identify Fourier coefficient
  differences with the true cover-difference operators including centered
  carries. <verbatim|bankCost> combines the finite type labels, and
  <verbatim|limited_lengths>/<verbatim|honored> uniformize all word lengths
  up to <verbatim|j-2> by finite minima of widths and maxima of costs. The
  order-two tower comes from <verbatim|Flight.duo.T = meshBook (wire k) 2 []
  P> in <verbatim|Lean/YM/Next/FREE_KERNELS/Primal.lean>; its base and
  first-difference costs are the <verbatim|L^-2> and <verbatim|L^-3> receipts
  used by the atlas producer.

  <section|Classification and remaining work><label|classification-and-remaining-work>

  The article now gives the finite train recursion and its quantitative
  history induction, including the coefficient-one principal-plank lower
  bound, geometric side-ratio lower estimate, inverse-square nozzle
  recursion, and successive reciprocal continuation for halo, lid, bulb, and
  bend. It also records the actual zero and nonzero branch identities and
  their exact matrix order in <verbatim|free-kernel-estimates.tex>; these
  calculations reorganize <verbatim|Flight.copper_match>,
  <verbatim|Flight.stake_match>, and <verbatim|Flight.wire_match>. The
  reciprocal overlap proof is written from the residue exchange and the
  two-variable script shift, corresponding to <verbatim|Flight.knots_shift>
  and <verbatim|Secured.relay>.

  The remaining work is to check these prose calculations against the source
  in independent review and integrate them with the actual selected-run
  chain. This chapter does not claim to prove the finite-scale funding or
  horizon response estimates used by the caller; those are separate
  construction inputs recorded in the RG construction unit.

